// WoSX's default tree: FCPW's scalar binary BVH in FCPW's GPU bytes
// (apps/rtq/schedules/layouts/fcpwb_gpu.bonsai: the triangles as GPUTriangle
// rows, the corners stored, where FCPW's CPU gathers them through its
// polygon soup -- the leaf-layout bias apps/rtq/PLAN.md describes; the CPU
// bytes are layouts/fcpwb.bonsai there, and this driver's switch to them is
// pending), the walk a loop, the closest-point traversal on
// a stack of FCPW's depth with the two children tested at their parent and
// the nearer entered first -- the children's boxes read from the children's
// rows, as the layout says -- and the leaf's Voronoi arms skipped where the
// point is not in them: the matching schedule against WoSX built as it
// ships (FCPW_USE_ENOKI off), one thread against one thread; the threads
// and the persistent workers (fcpwb-threads, fcpwb-persistent) import it.
// Nothing is vectorized: FCPW's scalar traversal tests one box and one
// triangle at a time, and so does this.
//
// The order is FCPW's CPU traversal's (bvh.inl, processSubtreeForClosest
// Point): the nearer child first by its squared distance to its box, d2Min
// -- and where both children contain the point, both d2Min zero, by the
// far corner's distance, d2Max, the nearer far corner first. A tie keeps
// the left child first, as FCPW's strict compares do. The key reads both
// children's boxes, which the children's tests read anyway. WoSX's GPU
// kernel orders by d2Min alone, and the GPU schedules take their traversal
// from kernel/fcpwb.bonsai for that.
//
// The two children's tests are written out (`unroll`), as FCPW writes them:
// the left child's box read off its row and tested, then the right's, each
// test tightening the far-corner bound; then the one comparison of the two
// keys and the swap, the farther pushed and the nearer entered
// (SSA/SortRecursion.cpp orders a run of two as scalars). Left a loop, the
// two tests were a loop of two over the pair of boxes the layout presents,
// each iteration picking its lane -- the sixty instructions over FCPW's
// visit that PLAN.md's "The binary CPU walk's gap" found (2026-10-24).
import apps/rtq/schedules/layouts/fcpwb_gpu;

schedule {
    walk.loopify();
    closest_within.sort(triangles.Interior,
                        |i : u32, s : Sphere, lo : vector[vec3f, 2], hi : vector[vec3f, 2]|
                        select(distmin(s, AABB{lo[0u], hi[0u]}) == 0.0 &&
                               distmin(s, AABB{lo[1u], hi[1u]}) == 0.0,
                               distmax(s, AABB{lo[i], hi[i]}),
                               distmin(s, AABB{lo[i], hi[i]})))
                  .unroll(triangles.Interior.children)
                  .loopify(64);
    closest_within.skip(closest_point_fcpw);
}
